messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the product problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of <<number1 such as 27>> and <<number2 such as 153>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. <<number2 153 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.>>\n1. <<Multiply number1 27 by the ones place digit 3 of number2 153>>\n2. <<Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153>>\n<<other digits of number2 if it has>>\nAnswer:\nNow, sum all the step results: <<sum of the results>>.\nSo, the final computed product is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 47982 and 12816?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\n\nFirst, I need to multiply 47,982 by 12,816. To make this manageable, I'll break down 12,816 into its place values: 10,000, 2,000, 800, 10, and 6.\n\nStarting with the largest place value, I'll multiply 47,982 by 10,000. This is straightforward since multiplying by 10,000 adds four zeros to the end of the number, resulting in 479,820,000.\n\nNext, I'll multiply 47,982 by 2,000. Multiplying by 2,000 is the same as multiplying by 2 and then adding three zeros. So, 47,982 multiplied by 2 is 95,964, and adding three zeros gives 95,964,000.\n\nThen, I'll multiply 47,982 by 800. Multiplying by 800 involves multiplying by 8 and then adding two zeros. 47,982 multiplied by 8 is 383,856, and adding two zeros results in 38,385,600.\n\nAfter that, I'll multiply 47,982 by 10. This is simple as it just adds a zero at the end, giving 479,820.\n\nFinally, I'll multiply 47,982 by 6, which equals 287,892.\n\nNow, I'll add all these partial products together to find the total sum.\n</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
